  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'schemabench_0011' consists of 9 tables. The main tables are 'ticketit_statuses', 'ticketit_priorities', 'ticketit_categories', 'ticketit', 'ticketit_comments', 'ticketit_audits', 'users', and 'ticketit_settings'.\nThe 'ticketit_statuses' table represents the status of a ticket, with columns for 'id', 'name', and 'color'. The 'ticketit_priorities' table represents the priority of a ticket, with columns for 'id', 'name', and 'color'. The 'ticketit_categories' table represents the categories of a ticket, with columns for 'id', 'name', and 'color'.\nThe 'ticketit_categories_users' table is a junction table that links users and categories, with columns for 'category_id' and 'user_id'. It has foreign keys referencing the 'users' and 'ticketit_categories' tables.\nThe 'ticketit' table represents the main ticket table, with columns for 'id', 'subject', 'content', 'html', 'status_id', 'priority_id', 'user_id', 'agent_id', 'category_id', 'created_at', 'updated_at', and 'completed_at'. It has foreign keys referencing the 'ticketit_statuses', 'users', 'ticketit_priorities', and 'ticketit_categories' tables.\nThe 'ticketit_comments' table represents the comments on tickets, with columns for 'id', 'user_id', 'ticket_id', 'created_at', 'updated_at', and 'content'. It has foreign keys referencing the 'users' and 'ticketit' tables.\nThe 'ticketit_audits' table represents the audits of tickets, with columns for 'id', 'operation', 'user_id', 'ticket_id', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'. It has foreign keys referencing the 'ticketit' and 'users' tables.\nThe 'users' table represents the users of the system, with columns for 'id', 'ticketit_admin', and 'ticketit_agent'.\nThe 'ticketit_settings' table represents the settings of the ticketit system, with columns for 'id', 'lang', 'slug', 'value', 'default', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'.\nThis schema ensures domain integrity by ensuring that all columns allow null values except for 'id' in all tables, and that 'id' is the primary key in all tables. It also ensures entity integrity by having foreign keys that reference the primary keys of other tables. The schema also allows for referential integrity by setting up cascade delete and update rules on foreign keys.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an open-source helpdesk ticketing system that seamlessly integrates with Laravel's default user authentication system. The solution supports Laravel versions 5.1 through 8. and can be easily integrated into existing Laravel projects. The helpdesk system aims to provide a simple and effective way to manage customer support tickets.\n\nObjectives\n\n1. To offer a straightforward integration of a helpdesk system within Laravel projects.\n2. To facilitate efficient ticket management for users, agents, and admins.\n3. To provide multilingual support using various language packs.\n4. To include analytics and performance tracking for administrative users.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n1. Users: Customers or team members who create and manage their support tickets.\n2. Agents: Staff responsible for handling and resolving tickets.\n3. Admins: Managers who oversee the ticketing process and system administration.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. User Roles:\n   - Users, agents, and admins have distinct roles with configurable access permissions.\n   \n2. Ticket Management:\n   - Users can create, track, comment on, and close their tickets.\n   - A text editor is available for ticket descriptions and comments, supporting image uploads.\n   \n3. Agent Assignment:\n   - The system automatically assigns tickets to agents based on departmental queues and agent load.\n   \n4. Localization:\n   - Support for multiple languages, including Arabic, Portuguese, German, English, Farsi, French, Hungarian, Italian, Persian, Russian, and Spanish.\n   \n5. Admin Dashboard:\n   - Features dashboards with statistics, graphical performance tracking, and simple administrative controls.\n\n6. Integration:\n   - Rapid installation and seamless integration with Laravel projects.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Performance:\n   - The system must efficiently manage ticket status and priority updates.\n   \n2. Usability:\n   - The interface should be user-friendly to ensure ease of use for all user roles.\n\n3. Scalability:\n   - The system should support a growing number of tickets and users without performance degradation.\n\n4. Security:\n   - Secure user authentication and data integrity must be maintained throughout ticket management.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0011.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0011\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_statuses` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(7) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_priorities` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(7) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_categories` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(7) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ticketit_admin` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `ticketit_agent` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subject` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `html` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `priority_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `agent_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `completed_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`status_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_statuses` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`priority_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_priorities` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_comments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `content` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_comments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_comments_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`ticket_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_audits` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `operation` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_audits_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_audits_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`ticket_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_categories_users` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`, `user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_categories_users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_categories_users_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_settings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `lang` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `value` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `default` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
